user:
---
The target function is `png_info_init_3`. `png_info_init_3` is a deprecated helper to initialize/reinitialize a `png_info` struct, using default allocation. If the passed buffer is too small, it frees it and allocates a new one; otherwise it zeroes the struct. Use newer libpng init APIs instead. Its signature is as follows:

```cpp
/* DEPRECATED: this function allowed init structures to be created using the
 * default allocation method (typically malloc).  Use is deprecated in 1.6.0 and
 * the API will be removed in the future.
 */
PNG_EXPORTA(19, void, png_info_init_3, (png_infopp info_ptr,
    size_t png_info_struct_size), PNG_DEPRECATED);
```

The list of candidate functions from the library is:

1. `png_benign_error`
    `png_benign_error` reports a non-fatal libpng problem. If `PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` is set, it warns; otherwise it treats it as an error. In read mode with a current chunk, it uses chunk-specific warn/error; else normal warn/error. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /* Benign error in libpng.  Can continue, but may have a problem.
 * User can choose whether to handle as a fatal error or as a warning. */
PNG_EXPORT(107, void, png_benign_error, (png_const_structrp png_ptr,
    png_const_charp warning_message));
    ```
2. `png_set_write_fn`
    `png_set_write_fn` installs custom write/flush callbacks and user I/O state for PNG output. Use it before writing; `io_ptr` is passed to callbacks. If `write_data_fn` is NULL, libpng uses its default write; flush may be NULL when unsupported/unused. If `png_ptr` is NULL, it returns immediately. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /* Replace the default data output functions with a user supplied one(s).
 * If buffered output is not used, then output_flush_fn can be set to NULL.
 * If PNG_WRITE_FLUSH_SUPPORTED is not defined at libpng compile time
 * output_flush_fn will be ignored (and thus can be NULL).
 * It is probably a mistake to use NULL for output_flush_fn if
 * write_data_fn is not also NULL unless you have built libpng with
 * PNG_WRITE_FLUSH_SUPPORTED undefined, because in this case libpng's
 * default flush function, which uses the standard *FILE structure, will
 * be used.
 */
PNG_EXPORT(77, void, png_set_write_fn, (png_structrp png_ptr, png_voidp io_ptr,
    png_rw_ptr write_data_fn, png_flush_ptr output_flush_fn));
    ```
3. `png_set_shift`
    `png_set_shift` enables the PNG_SHIFT transform and stores the “true” bit depths in `png_ptr->shift`. Use it before reading/conversion to expand low-bit-depth samples to legal depths. If `png_ptr` is NULL, it does nothing. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /* Converts files to legal bit depths. */
PNG_EXPORT(44, void, png_set_shift, (png_structrp png_ptr, png_const_color_8p
    true_bits));
    ```
4. `png_create_write_struct`
    Creates and initializes a `png_struct` for PNG writing. Use it first, passing libpng version and optional error/warn callbacks. If user memory support is off, it uses default allocation; otherwise it delegates to `png_create_write_struct_2`. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /* Allocate and initialize png_ptr struct for writing, and any other memory */
PNG_EXPORTA(5, png_structp, png_create_write_struct,
    (png_const_charp user_png_ver, png_voidp error_ptr, png_error_ptr error_fn,
    png_error_ptr warn_fn),
    PNG_ALLOCATED);
    ```
5. `png_write_info`
    `png_write_info` starts PNG output: it writes the IHDR-related info and any required chunks (e.g. PLTE, tRNS, text) from `info_ptr`. Call it after setting up `png_ptr/info_ptr`, before writing image rows. NULL args return; missing PLTE for palette images is an error. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(21, void, png_write_info,
    (png_structrp png_ptr, png_const_inforp info_ptr));
    ```
6. `png_get_x_pixels_per_inch`
    Returns the image X resolution in pixels/inch, derived from the stored X pixels/meter value. Use it after reading metadata via `png_ptr`/`info_ptr`. If no X pHYs data is present, it returns the converted value from that field’s default/zero state. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(194, png_uint_32, png_get_x_pixels_per_inch,
    (png_const_structrp png_ptr, png_const_inforp info_ptr));
    ```
7. `png_get_unknown_chunks`
    Returns the count of unknown chunks in `info_ptr` and sets `*unknowns` to the chunk array. Use only with non-NULL `png_ptr`, `info_ptr`, and output pointer. If any arg is NULL, it returns 0 and leaves nothing set. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(176, int, png_get_unknown_chunks, (png_const_structrp png_ptr,
    png_inforp info_ptr, png_unknown_chunkpp entries));
    ```
8. `png_set_error_fn`
    Sets libpng’s per-`png_struct` error/warning callbacks and user error context. Call it after creating `png_ptr`. If `png_ptr==NULL`, it does nothing. Stores `error_ptr`; `warning_fn` is saved only when warnings are enabled. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(75, void, png_set_error_fn, (png_structrp png_ptr,
    png_voidp error_ptr, png_error_ptr error_fn, png_error_ptr warning_fn));
    ```
9. `png_set_quantize`
    `png_set_quantize` enables PNG quantization (`PNG_QUANTIZE`) to reduce a palette to `maximum_colors`. If `full_quantize==0`, it builds a direct index map; otherwise it uses full quantization. If `num_palette>maximum_colors` and `histogram` is given, least-used colors are discarded. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /* Turn on quantizing, and reduce the palette to the number of colors
 * available.
 */
PNG_EXPORT(49, void, png_set_quantize, (png_structrp png_ptr,
    png_colorp palette, int num_palette, int maximum_colors,
    png_const_uint_16p histogram, int full_quantize));
    ```
10. `png_set_PLTE`
    `png_set_PLTE` stores a PLTE palette in `info_ptr` for indexed-color PNGs. Use it with a valid `png_ptr/info_ptr`, a palette array, and count. For palette images, count must be ≤ `1<<bit_depth`; otherwise ≤ `PNG_MAX_PALETTE_LENGTH`. Invalid length errors on palette images, warns/returns on others. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(152, void, png_set_PLTE, (png_structrp png_ptr,
    png_inforp info_ptr, png_const_colorp palette, int num_palette));
    ```
11. `png_get_user_height_max`
    Returns the current per-image height limit set in `png_ptr->user_height_max`. Use it to query the user-defined maximum height. If `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it returns `0`. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(188, png_uint_32, png_get_user_height_max,
    (png_const_structrp png_ptr));
    ```
12. `png_set_read_status_fn`
    Sets a callback for read progress. Call it with a valid `png_structrp`; passing `NULL` does nothing. The function simply stores `read_row_fn` in `png_ptr`, so later row-reading code can invoke it. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(80, void, png_set_read_status_fn, (png_structrp png_ptr,
    png_read_status_ptr read_row_fn));
    ```
13. `png_get_header_version`
    Returns a compile-time header version string, not tied to `png_ptr` (it’s unused). Use it to query the libpng header version/date. If `__STDC__` is defined, it appends newline and, when read support is off, “(NO READ SUPPORT)”. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(182, png_const_charp, png_get_header_version,
    (png_const_structrp png_ptr));
    ```
14. `png_save_uint_16`
    `png_save_uint_16` stores a 16-bit unsigned value into a 2-byte buffer in big-endian/network order. Use it when writing PNG data fields. It always writes `buf[0]` = high byte, `buf[1]` = low byte; caller must provide at least 2 bytes. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(207, void, png_save_uint_16, (png_bytep buf, unsigned int i));
    ```
15. `png_set_compression_method`
    Sets the PNG zlib compression method in `png_ptr`. Use it on a valid `png_structrp`; if `png_ptr` is NULL, it returns. Any `method` other than 8 triggers a warning, but the value is still stored. PNG supports only method 8. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_EXPORT(73, void, png_set_compression_method, (png_structrp png_ptr,
    int method));
    ```
16. `png_get_pixel_aspect_ratio_fixed`
    Returns the pixel aspect ratio as a fixed-point value from pHYs: `y_pixels_per_unit / x_pixels_per_unit`. Use only when `png_ptr/info_ptr` are valid and pHYs exists; otherwise it returns 0. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    
PNG_FIXED_EXPORT(210, png_fixed_point, png_get_pixel_aspect_ratio_fixed,
    (png_const_structrp png_ptr, png_const_inforp info_ptr))

/* Returns image x, y offset in pixels or microns, from oFFs chunk data. */
PNG_EXPORT(126, png_int_32, png_get_x_offset_pixels,
    (png_const_structrp png_ptr, png_const_inforp info_ptr));
    ```
17. `png_get_y_offset_inches`
