## Target API Functions

1. `png_get_chunk_cache_max`
​ Returns the user-set maximum number of unknown chunks libpng will cache. Use it with a valid `png_ptr`; if `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it returns `0`. It only reports `png_ptr->user_chunk_cache_max`, no other behavior.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
PNG_EXPORT(190, png_uint_32, png_get_chunk_cache_max,
 (png_const_structrp png_ptr));
​ ```

2. `png_get_user_width_max`
​ Returns the user-set maximum image width from `png_ptr`. Use it to query the limit previously configured in the PNG read/write struct. If `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it returns `0`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
PNG_EXPORT(187, png_uint_32, png_get_user_width_max,
 (png_const_structrp png_ptr));
​ ```

3. `png_permit_mng_features`
​ Sets which MNG features libpng may accept. Call with a valid `png_ptr` and a bitmask; it stores only `PNG_ALL_MNG_FEATURES` bits and returns the permitted mask. If `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it returns 0 and changes nothing.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
PNG_EXPORT(184, png_uint_32, png_permit_mng_features, (png_structrp png_ptr, png_uint_32 mng_features_permitted));
​ ```

4. `png_set_user_limits`
​ Sets per-decoder max image width/height. Call it on a valid `png_structrp` before `png_set_IHDR()`. Images exceeding either limit are rejected; use `0x7fffffff` for “accept any” size. If `png_ptr == NULL`, it does nothing.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
PNG_EXPORT(186, void, png_set_user_limits, (png_structrp png_ptr, png_uint_32 user_width_max, png_uint_32 user_height_max));
​ ```

5. `png_set_chunk_cache_max`
​ `png_set_chunk_cache_max` sets `png_ptr->user_chunk_cache_max`, i.e. the maximum number of chunks libpng will cache. Call it on a valid `png_structrp` before reading. If `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it does nothing.
