2. `png_set_bgr`
​ `png_set_bgr` enables the `PNG_BGR` transform, telling libpng to treat pixel bytes as BGR instead of RGB. Call it on a valid `png_structrp` before reading/writing image data. If `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it does nothing.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
/* Use blue, green, red order for pixels. */
PNG_EXPORT(30, void, png_set_bgr, (png_structrp png_ptr));
​ ```

3. `png_set_strip_alpha`
​ `png_set_strip_alpha` enables the `PNG_STRIP_ALPHA` transform. Call it on a valid `png_structrp` before reading rows to drop any alpha channel. If read transforms are no longer allowed (`png_rtran_ok` fails), it does nothing.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
PNG_EXPORT(36, void, png_set_strip_alpha, (png_structrp png_ptr));
​ ```

4. `png_start_read_image`
​ Starts row/image read setup. Call once before reading image data, typically after `png_read_update_info`. If row init hasn’t run, it calls `png_read_start_row`; if called twice, it reports a duplicate-call app error.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
/* Optional update palette with requested transformations */
PNG_EXPORT(53, void, png_start_read_image, (png_structrp png_ptr));
​ ```

5. `png_set_invert_mono`
​ `png_set_invert_mono` enables the `PNG_INVERT_MONO` transform on a valid `png_ptr`. Use it before reading/converting image data to flip monochrome bit meaning. If `png_ptr` is `NULL`, it does nothing.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
/* Invert monochrome files */
PNG_EXPORT(46, void, png_set_invert_mono, (png_structrp png_ptr));
​ ```

6. `png_set_palette_to_rgb`
​ `png_set_palette_to_rgb` requests libpng to expand paletted images to full RGB, and also expand tRNS transparency if present. Call it before reading image rows. If transformations are already locked out, it does nothing; otherwise it sets `PNG_EXPAND | PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`.
